angle.hop-across-cut.formal:
On two modes, let $H = 7N_{0} - 12(a^{\dagger}_{0}a_{1} + a^{\dagger}_{1}a_{0})$, the second quantisation of the one-particle operator $\begin{psmallmatrix} 7 & -12 \\ -12 & 0\end{psmallmatrix}$, which is not in the supplied class.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $H$ is Hermitian and conserves the total number; its spectrum is $0$ on $\ket{00}$, $7$ on $\ket{11}$, and $16, -9$ on the one-particle sector, so its ground state is unique: $\psi = \tfrac35\ket{10} + \tfrac45\ket{01}$ with eigenvalue $-9$.
\item[(ii)] $\psi$ is an odd vector, hence of definite parity, so the state it defines is even (it vanishes on the odd sector) and is physical under the univalence rule, and entangled across the cut: its left marginal is $\operatorname{diag}(\tfrac{16}{25}, \tfrac{9}{25})$, the Gibbs state for $N_{0}$ at $e^{-\omega/T} = \tfrac{9}{16}$, the singularity paper's sample point, and its right marginal is $\operatorname{diag}(\tfrac{9}{25}, \tfrac{16}{25})$.
\item[(iii)] $\psi$ is an eigenvector of $N_{0} + N_{1}$ and not of $N_{0} - N_{1}$: it is invariant under the boost flow in which the two modes have equal frequency, and not under the number-difference boost of the vacuum form.
\end{enumerate}

__notes__.note.pairing.setting-what-does-two-claim:
	extbf{Verdict on Claim R2.} K2.1 does not trigger: the classes of Propositions \claimref{pairing.static-frame-only-expansion-survives} and \claimref{pairing.regular-frame-class-vanishes-horizon-changes} are nonzero off the horizon. K2.2 does not trigger: the class is $\ip{
abla_{X}\ell}{m}$, its zeros are invariant under rescaling, and the two frames agree covariantly. K2.3 does not trigger: the classes along $\partial_{t}$ and $\partial_{r}$ vanish in both frames. K2.4 triggers: every generator found acts on the two modes of one fibre, and the object its wording asks for, a generator in $\so(V_{x} \oplus V_{y})$ pairing the completion's independent ladders at two points, is not something a connection produces. So Claim R2 is closed for the pre-ascent Dirac fibre and open for the boundary modes across points. Of the predictions, P2.2 and P2.4 held, P2.1 held with its two clauses in two frames, and P2.3 was refuted as worded (Remark \claimref{note.pairing.departures-registered-wording}).

How much was at risk should be said plainly: nothing. K2.2 is an identity, since $\Omega$ is defined by $
abla_{X}v = 	frac12[\Omega(X), v]$ and $\pv$ by $\ip{B\cdot\ell}{m}$, so $\pv(\Omega(X)) = \ip{
abla_{X}\ell}{m}$ could not have failed. K2.1 asks whether the outgoing radial congruence of Schwarzschild expands. K2.3 proposes to contradict an ascended statement about the completed pair by a pre-ascent exterior computation that cannot reach it. K2.4 was registered as expected to trigger, and did. The apparatus adjudicated nothing on this claim; what the registration did was fix the computation and its sign conventions before the numbers existed. The content of the note is the dictionary and the exact numbers.

angle.hop-sea-filled-pairing.formal:
Let $V = (a_{1} + a^{\dagger}_{1})\Gamma_{0}$ on the pair, with $\Gamma_{0} = (-1)^{N_{0}}$ and $\ket{11} = a^{\dagger}_{0}a^{\dagger}_{1}\ket{00}$. Then $V$ is unitary and Hermitian, $V^{2} = 1$, conjugation by $V$ fixes $a_{0}$ and exchanges $a_{1}$ with $a^{\dagger}_{1}$ (the particle-hole automorphism of the right mode), and:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $V\psi = \tfrac45\ket{00} + \tfrac35\ket{11}$, exactly the singularity paper's thermofield pair at the sample point, an even state with the same left marginal $\operatorname{diag}(\tfrac{16}{25}, \tfrac{9}{25})$;
\item[(ii)] $VHV^{-1} = 7N_{0} - 12(a^{\dagger}_{0}a^{\dagger}_{1} + a_{1}a_{0})$, a pairing generator with the same spectrum, whose unique ground state is $V\psi$;
\item[(iii)] $V(N_{0} + N_{1} - 1)V^{-1} = N_{0} - N_{1}$: the equal-frequency boost of the hop form becomes the number-difference boost of the vacuum form.
\end{enumerate}

angle.within-supplied-class-spectrum-con.formal:
Let $H$ be a generator in the supplied class on $\nu$ modes, a polynomial in the number operators, and let a cut divide the modes into two sides.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $H$ is diagonal in the joint number basis. If its ground state is unique, that state is a number basis state: a product across the cut, with a pure marginal on each side, an eigenvector of every $N_{k}$ and hence of every boost generator $\sum_{k}\omega_{k}(N_{k} - \tfrac12)$, and Gibbs on a side at no finite $\beta$: a Boulware-type state, a product with pure marginals.
\item[(ii)] If the ground state is not unique, the ground space may contain entangled states, but the spectrum condition selects none of them: for $H = (N_{0} - N_{1})^{2} = N_{0} + N_{1} - 2N_{0}N_{1}$ on the pair, the ground space is $\operatorname{span}\{\ket{00}, \ket{11}\}$, which contains the thermofield pair $\tfrac45\ket{00} + \tfrac35\ket{11}$ and the product state $\ket{00}$ alike.
\end{enumerate}
Within the supplied class, therefore, positivity of the energy selects a Boulware-type state or selects no state, and Claim G is dead at the state-selection step.

hawking.homogeneity-ordering-shift.technical:
Realise the palatial relation $[Z^{\mu},  ar Z_{
u}] = \hbar\,\delta^{\mu}_{
u}$ on functions on the conformal spinor space $\CS$ by $ ar Z_{
u} = -\hbar\,\partial/\partial Z^{
u}$, let $\Upsilon = Z^{\mu}\partial_{\mu}$ be the Euler operator with eigenvalue $\mathsf{h}$, and let the helicity operator be $\mathsf{s} = 	frac12 q(\id)$ in the symmetric ordering. Then $q(\id) = -\hbar(\Upsilon + 2)$ as an operator identity, so a function homogeneous of degree $\mathsf{h}$ has helicity $s\hbar$ exactly when $\mathsf{h} = -2s - 2$.

The offset $-2$ is precisely the symmetric-ordering shift of $-\hbar/2$ per complex dimension of $\CS$, the complex unit being $I_6$; without it the offset would vanish. Combined with the Penrose transform, this makes the transform's classification parameter the completion's own $\mathfrak{u}(1)$ charge.

hawking.i-state-continuum.formal:
For the continuum mode space at $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa$, with $h$ self-adjoint:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the quasi-free state $\omega_I$ with symbol $A_I = (1 + e^{\beta h})^{-1}$ is $\tau$-KMS at $\beta$: the two-point KMS identity is the computation of clause (3) run in reverse on the core, and Fact \claimref{hawking.imported-quasi-free-kms-reduces-two} lifts it from the two-point level to the state. When $h$ has pure point spectrum, the finite spectral windows (spans of finitely many eigenvectors) are literal instances of the section “The state input: the I-state”'s exact window theorem, proved outright; this is the case of the tower's mode space, Definition \claimref{obj.hawking.boundary-mode-algebra-windows-boost-dynamics};
\item the pairing of any quasi-free $\tau$-KMS state vanishes, by the computation of Theorem \claimref{hawking.i-state}(2) performed on the core of clause (3);
\item the symbol derivation holds verbatim on the core: for $f$ in a bounded spectral subspace of $h$ (their union over cut-offs is a dense, $\tau$-invariant core on which $e^{-\beta h}f$ is defined and $a^\dagger(f)$ is entire analytic for $\tau$), the KMS identity forces $\ip{g}{A(1 + e^{-\beta h})f} = \ip{g}{e^{-\beta h}f}$, hence $A = A_I$ on the core and, $A$ and $A_I$ both bounded, everywhere: within the quasi-free class the continuum I-state is unique with derived two-point data. ($e^{-\beta h}$ may be unbounded when $h$ is unbounded below; the identity is only ever used on the core, and $A_I$ is bounded for every self-adjoint $h$.)
\end{enumerate}
Fact \claimref{hawking.imported-ideal-fermi-gas-uniqueness} is stated for general self-adjoint $h$, so Corollary \claimref{hawking.full-uniqueness-given-dynamics} also holds verbatim for the continuum mode space: among all states, $\omega_I$ is the unique $\tau$-KMS state.

hawking.imported-twistor-storey-ordering.technical:
The spinor module of the completed algebra $\Cl(2,4)$ is the conformal spinor space $\CS$, of dimension $4$ over the complex unit $I_6$. Its quadratic (palatial) quantisation carries $[Z^{\mu},  ar Z_{
u}] = \hbar\,\delta^{\mu}_{
u}$, with $ ar Z$ dual to $Z$ under the reversion form, and for $M \in \mathfrak{gl}(\CS)$ the symmetrised quadratic is $q(M) = 	frac12(Z^{\mu}M_{\mu}{}^{
u} ar Z_{
u} +  ar Z_{
u}M_{\mu}{}^{
u}Z^{\mu})$.

Symmetric ordering shifts the distinguished $\mathfrak{u}(1)$ generator $q(\id)$ by $-\hbar/2$ per complex dimension, $-2\hbar$ in total; equivalently $q(M) = \sum M_{\mu}{}^{
u}Z^{\mu} ar Z_{
u} - 	frac{\hbar}{2}	r M$. On the Clifford storey the boost generator of each adjoined plane is $N - 	frac12$, a shift of minus one half per fermionic mode, so the two storeys carry the same signed ordering shift.

singularity.modular-flow-boundary-modes-.formal:
In the collar of Fact \claimref{singularity.imported-interior-collar-kasner-form}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the transport along a $t$-line at fixed $r$ is a boost in the plane $B_1$ of the completed pair, with rapidity per unit Killing time $|A'(r)|/2 = 1/(2r^2)$ (in general units $M/r^2$), an identity of rational functions; at the horizon $r = 1$ it equals $\tfrac12 = \kappa$, the rate of Proposition \claimref{singularity.plane-rate}, so the modular flow of Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-structure-completed-pair} with the period of Premise \claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus} is the $t$-line holonomy at the horizon; inside, the rate grows as $r^{-2}$ and the Killing-time KMS period $2\pi/(|A'|/2) = 4\pi r^2$ shrinks with it;
\item[(ii)] Hubble-normalised, the same rate is $\tau\,|A'|/(2\sqrt{A}) = \tfrac13 S(r)/\sqrt{1 - r} = \tfrac13 + \tfrac{4}{15}r + \cdots$ with $\tau = \tfrac23 r^{3/2} S(r)$, whose limit at the exceptional set is $|p_1| = \tfrac13$; the period $2\pi$ of the boost's rapidity divided by this limit is
\[
\beta_{\mathrm{H}} \;=\; \frac{2\pi}{|p_1|} \;=\; 6\pi ,
\]
independent of $M$ and of the position on the exceptional set. If the KMS state of the boundary modes on the completed pair extends to a KMS state of the $t$-line holonomy flow on the collar, then its inverse temperature in Hubble-normalised time at the exceptional set is this $\beta_{\mathrm{H}}$; whether it so extends is open (the section “What is established, what is premised, what is open”);
\item[(iii)] for the sphere directions the corresponding number would be $2\pi/p_2 = 3\pi$. The limiting boosts $B_1$, $B_2$, $B_3$ pairwise fail to commute, $B_1 \times B_2 = -R_{12} \neq 0$ (commutator product, $A \times B = (AB - BA)/2$), so no two of the three limiting flows have proportional generators. Consequently, on any von Neumann algebra carrying the three flows as automorphism groups, a faithful normal state that is KMS at some $\beta \neq 0$ for one of them is KMS at no $\beta' \neq 0$ for either other, by the uniqueness of the modular group; at $\beta = 0$ a trace is KMS for every flow, and is excluded. This series constructs no such algebra for the collar; on the one algebra this paper does construct, the matrix factor of Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-structure-completed-pair}, only $B_1$ acts. The direction the modular flow of the boundary modes selects is $B_1$, the $t$-direction, by (i).
\end{enumerate}

state-selection.euclidean-partner-curvat.formal:
Let $\sigma_r$ be the unit boost bivector of the radial plane and $I$ the pseudoscalar. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $(I\sigma_r)^{2} = -1$, and $\{\sigma_r,\, I\sigma_r\}$ is precisely the doubled eigenplane pair of the shape operator, $S(B) = -2B$ on it, of \cite[Thm.~6.1]{ButlerT5};
\item the rotor $R_\theta := \exp(\theta\, I\sigma_r/2) = \cos(\theta/2) + I\sigma_r \sin(\theta/2)$ satisfies $R_{2\pi} = -1$ and $R_{4\pi} = +1$, so the rotor has period $4\pi$ while its conjugation action $x \mapsto R_\theta x R_\theta^{-1}$ has period $2\pi$;
\item $R_\theta$ is the analytic continuation of the boost rotor $\exp(\lambda\sigma_r/2)$ under $\lambda \mapsto i\theta$ in the sense that both are one-parameter rotor flows in the same eigenplane pair, the hyperbolic one generated by the member with square $+1$ and the elliptic one by the member with square $-1$.
\item On spinors, each chiral half: with the unit of the continuation $\lambda \mapsto i\theta$ read as the conformal spinors' unit $I_{6} = I_{4}E$ (with $I_{4} = I$ and $E = e_{+}e_{-}$ the bivector of the added plane), the continued boost acts on the two chiral halves of the conformal spinors as $R_\theta$ and $R_{-\theta}$, the sense fixed by the chirality (Proposition \claimref{synthesis.wick-rotor-chiral-angular-rotor}(ii)).
\end{enumerate}
In its action on spinors, on each chiral half, the Euclidean rotation whose angle carries the periodicity is therefore not an imported device: it is the second member of the pair that Step 4b's curvature equation already selects, and it is distinguished from the boost by exactly the sign of its square, the same criterion \cite[Cor.~6.4]{ButlerT5} uses to pick out $\sigma_r$. On vectors, and for every holonomy of a real connection, it is not: there $R_\theta$ rotates along the bifurcation sphere, not around it, and the Euclidean rotation around the sphere is realised by no real rotor of $\Cl(1,3)$ (Proposition \claimref{singularity.euclidean-rotor-where-it-lies}(3)).

synthesis.array-ln2-is-zero-mode-doublet.formal:
Assume (Q) (Premise \claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave}) as the bridge does, so that one boundary-mode array $\{\psi_{k}\}_{k \in \Z}$ of Theorem \claimref{singularity.bosonisation-bridge-s-wave-sector} has weights $\kappa k$, sea filled below $k = 0$, and is in the Gibbs state at $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa$. Measure charge $Q$ and energy $E$ (in units of $\kappa$) from the sea. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (charge times one tower) the configurations of charge $Q$ and energy $E$ are counted by $p(E - Q(Q-1)/2)$, with $p$ the partition function; equivalently the Fock space is $\bigoplus_{Q \in \Z}\mathcal{H}_{Q} \otimes \mathcal{F}$, where $\mathcal{H}_{Q}$ is one state of energy $Q(Q-1)/2$ and $\mathcal{F}$ is the Fock space of a single oscillator tower, one oscillator of energy $m$ for each $m \ge 1$, and the Gibbs state is the product of a charge distribution $P(Q) \propto e^{-2\pi Q(Q-1)/2}$ with the thermal state of the tower. Hence the array's thermal entropy splits exactly as $S_{\mathrm{array}} = S_{\mathrm{charge}} + S_{\mathrm{osc}}$ with $S_{\mathrm{osc}} = \sum_{m \ge 1}s_{\mathrm{B}}(2\pi m)$, $s_{\mathrm{B}}(x) = x/(e^{x} - 1) - \ln(1 - e^{-x})$;
\item[(ii)] (the doublet, and its dependence on the offset) exactly two configurations have zero energy, of charges $0$ and $1$ (the sea, and the sea with the zero mode filled), so $S_{\mathrm{charge}} > \ln 2$: the $\ln 2$ that Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}(vi) assigns to the zero-weight mode of an array is the two-fold degeneracy of this charge doublet, and the charge sector carries that $\ln 2$ plus a positive excess from the higher charges. The doublet belongs to (Q)'s offset (sea at a site). For the other particle-hole symmetric offset, weights $\kappa(k + \tfrac12)$ with the sea between sites, the same count gives ground energies $Q^{2}/2$, the same oscillator tower, and a unique zero-energy configuration: no doublet (the classification of the two offsets is filed alongside, synthesis.q-splits-maximum-entropy-affine-weights);
\item[(iii)] (no current with $m \neq 0$ connects it) every current commutes with the total charge; every $a_{m}$ with $m \neq 0$ acts on the tower factor $\mathcal{F}$ alone and has no matrix element between the two doublet states, and no element of the current algebra carries one doublet state to the other, since they differ in charge and are related by a fermion. The charge current $a_{0}$, the normal-ordered total charge, acts on the charge factor: it reads the doublet (eigenvalues $0$ and $1$) without changing it. In particular the Bose-Einstein occupancy of the bridge's clause (3) and the current boson that Joint J3 (Proposition \claimref{hawking.helicity-lift-joints-itemised}) identifies with the s-wave of a four-dimensional field (Fact \claimref{hawking.imported-s-wave-bosonic-sector}) carry $S_{\mathrm{osc}}$ only;
\item[(iv)] (one tower, so one channel) at each boost weight $m\kappa$ the current sector has exactly one oscillator: the charge-$0$ level counts are $p(E) = 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 11$ for $E = 0, \ldots, 6$, whereas two independent towers, which is what both helicities of one $(\omega, \ell, m)$ partial wave are, give $1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 36, 65$. So one array can register at most one helicity channel of the lift; two helicities need two arrays.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, in the arrays reading of the residue, the two-fold degeneracy that supplies the per-array $\ln 2$ lies in the charge sector, exists only at (Q)'s offset, and does not depend on how Joint J3 assigns helicities; the current boson carries $S_{\mathrm{osc}}$ only (its value, computed in the proof, is a small fraction of $\ln 2$). At the other offset the array carries $\tfrac12\ln 2$ (filed alongside, synthesis.half-integer-weight-array-carries-half-ln2). Nothing here identifies one registered mode with two arrays or with two helicities.

synthesis.boundary-mode-locality-is-desc.formal:
Let $v_{1}, \dots, v_{n}$ satisfy the CAR (a finite window of boundary modes), $\sigma_{k} = v_{k}^{*}v_{k} - v_{k}v_{k}^{*} = 2N_{k} - 1$ (Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-structure-completed-pair}), $\mathcal{D}$ the algebra generated by the $\sigma_{k}$, and $E_{k}(x) = N_{k}xN_{k} + (1 - N_{k})x(1 - N_{k})$ the descent of mode $k$ (Proposition \claimref{singularity.descent-what-it-erases}). Put $\mathcal{E} = E_{1}\circ\cdots\circ E_{n}$, the descent of the window. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\mathcal{E}(x) = \sum_{\nu}P_{\nu}xP_{\nu}$, the sum over the $2^{n}$ joint-number projectors $P_{\nu} = \prod_{k}(N_{k}\ \text{or}\ 1 - N_{k})$; it is an idempotent conditional expectation onto $\mathcal{D}$, which is the joint-number-diagonal algebra, of dimension $2^{n}$;
\item[(ii)] for every element $H$ of the window algebra, $\mathcal{E}\circ\operatorname{ad}_{H} = \operatorname{ad}_{H}\circ\mathcal{E}$ if and only if $H \in \mathcal{D}$; hence a one-parameter group $\alpha_{t} = \operatorname{Ad}(e^{itH})$ commutes with $\mathcal{E}$ for all $t$ if and only if $H \in \mathcal{D}$;
\item[(iii)] consequently the condition of Premise \claimref{singularity.l-boundary-mode-locality} on a window, that the internal dynamics be generated by elements of the algebra of the $\sigma_{k}$, is equivalent to
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(T)] the internal dynamics of the boundary modes commutes with the descent: $\alpha_{t}\circ\mathcal{E} = \mathcal{E}\circ\alpha_{t}$ for all $t$;
\end{enumerate}
hopping $v_{j}^{*}v_{k} + v_{k}^{*}v_{j}$, pairing $v_{j}^{*}v_{k}^{*} + v_{k}v_{j}$ and every generator with a component outside $\mathcal{D}$ fail (T), each witnessed by an occupation projector $N_{j}$ with $\mathcal{E}(\operatorname{ad}_{H}N_{j}) = 0 \neq \operatorname{ad}_{H}N_{j}$, while diagonal products such as $\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}$ satisfy it;
\item[(iv)] for channels the same covariance admits greybody filtering: a beam splitter of transmission $c^{2}$ between a mode and a vacuum in-mode gives on the transmitted mode the channel $\Phi\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}a & b\\ \bar b & d\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}a + s^{2}d & cb\\ c\bar b & c^{2}d\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $c^{2} + s^{2} = 1$, which satisfies $\Phi\circ E = E\circ\Phi$ and carries the occupation $N \mapsto c^{2}N$.
\end{enumerate}
So on every finite window, (L$_{E}$) is the statement that dynamics and descent commute for all $t$, with one clause for hopping, pairing and unsupplied generators alike; commuting at a single time is weaker, since permutations of the joint-number basis commute with $\mathcal{E}$.

