synthesis.collar-kms-extension-completio.formal:
Work in units $2M = 1$ on the collar of Fact \claimref{singularity.imported-interior-collar-kasner-form}, with $\kappa = \tfrac12$, $A = 1/r - 1$, and the rates of Proposition \claimref{singularity.modular-flow-boundary-modes-holonomy-universal}: $\rho(r) = 1/(2r^{2})$ per unit Killing time and, for $r < 1$, $\rho_{\mathrm{H}}(r) = \tfrac13 S(r)(1-r)^{-1/2} = \tfrac13 + \tfrac{4}{15}r + \cdots$ per unit Hubble-normalised parameter. By Fact \claimref{singularity.imported-one-sided-completion-singularity} the ladder pair of the completion exists exactly over the exceptional set $E = \{\tau = 0\}\times\R_{t}\times S^{2}$, and each line of constant $(t, \theta, \varphi)$ in the collar is a $\tau$-line ending at the point $(t, \theta, \varphi)$ of $E$. Assume:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(E)] the boundary-mode atom read at a collar point is the atom (the completed pair of Theorem \claimref{singularity.modular-structure-completed-pair}) of the point of $E$ on its $\tau$-line, carried along that line by the parallel transport of the synchronous frame; on a finite set $P \subset E$ the algebra is the CAR algebra of the atoms at $P$, a matrix factor; and the $t$-line holonomy flow of the slice $r$ acts on it as the phase flow $\operatorname{Ad}(e^{isf(r)K_{P}})$ with $K_{P} = \sum_{p \in P}K_{p}$, $K_{p} = \sigma_{p}/2$, where $f = \rho$ (the Killing parametrisation) or $f = \rho_{\mathrm{H}}$ (the Hubble parametrisation, $r < 1$).
\end{enumerate}
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the Killing bivector of $\partial_{t}$ at a collar point of the slice $r$ is $\rho(r)B_{1}$ with $B_{1} = e_{0}\wedge e_{1}$, and $B_{1}$ is parallel along $\partial_{\tau}$; so every slice carries to $E$ a positive multiple of the one generator $K_{P}$, with a factor that depends on $r$ only;
\item[(ii)] the product state $\omega_{P}$ with density $\bigotimes_{p}e^{-2\pi K_{p}}/\Tr(\cdot)$, the completed pair's state of rapidity period $2\pi$ at every point of $P$, is for every slice $r$ the unique KMS state of that slice's Killing flow at $\beta = 2\pi/\rho(r) = 4\pi r^{2}$ and of its Hubble flow at $\beta_{\mathrm{H}}(r) = 2\pi/\rho_{\mathrm{H}}(r) = 6\pi\sqrt{1-r}/S(r) = 6\pi(1 - \tfrac45 r + O(r^{2}))$; a state KMS for one slice's flow at some $\beta \neq 0$ is KMS for every other slice's flow at the rescaled inverse temperature; in Killing time the same state has $\beta = 4\pi r^{2} \to 0$ at the exceptional set (infinite Killing-time temperature there), so $6\pi$ is the value in Hubble-normalised time;
\item[(iii)] at the exceptional set the Hubble-normalised holonomy flow has rate $|p_{1}| = \tfrac13$, and $\omega_{P}$ is its unique KMS state at $\beta_{\mathrm{H}} = 6\pi$; in particular the horizon's KMS state extends, uniquely, to a KMS state of the $t$-line holonomy flow at the exceptional set, and the conditional sentence of Proposition \claimref{singularity.modular-flow-boundary-modes-holonomy-universal}(ii) holds under (E);
\item[(iv)] the running $\rho_{\mathrm{H}}(r) = \tfrac13 + \tfrac{4}{15}r + \cdots$ of the rate is under (E) a slice-dependent reparametrisation of one flow and obstructs nothing; by contrast, if two slices $r_{1} \neq r_{2}$ carried independent atoms (the slice-product reading), the product of period-$2\pi$ states would be KMS for no Killing-parametrised flow $\rho(r_{1})K_{1} + \rho(r_{2})K_{2}$ at any $\beta \neq 0$, since $\rho(r_{1}) \neq \rho(r_{2})$.
\end{enumerate}
Clause (i) is the evidence for the flow form assumed in (E); given that form, (ii) and (iii) are reparametrisation. So whether the extension exists is decided by the collar algebra: on the exceptional set's own modes, carried along the $\tau$-lines, it exists and gives $6\pi$; on independent slice atoms it does not.

synthesis.collar-kms-extension-three-flo.formal:
Work in units $2M = 1$ on the collar of Fact \claimref{singularity.imported-interior-collar-kasner-form}, with $\kappa = \tfrac12$, $A = 1/r - 1$, and $\tau(r) = \int_{0}^{r}\sqrt{x/(1-x)}\,dx = \tfrac23 r^{3/2}S(r)$. By Proposition \claimref{singularity.modular-flow-boundary-modes-holonomy-universal} the $t$-line holonomy on the slice $r$ is the $B_{1}$ boost of that slice's completed pair, with rapidity rate $\rho(r) = 1/(2r^{2})$ per unit Killing time and, for $r < 1$, $\rho_{\mathrm{H}}(r) = \tau\,|A'|/(2\sqrt{A}) = \tfrac13 S(r)(1-r)^{-1/2}$ per unit Hubble-normalised parameter. Assume:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(C)] for every finite set of slices $0 < r_{1} < \dots < r_{m} \le 1$ the collar algebra is the tensor product of the slices' matrix-factor atoms, each holonomy flow acting slice by slice as the phase flow $\operatorname{Ad}(e^{it f(r_{k})K_{k}})$ of its rate $f$, with $K_{k} = \sigma_{k}/2$; for the Hubble flow the slices satisfy $r_{m} < 1$, since $\rho_{\mathrm{H}}$ is not defined at the horizon.
\end{enumerate}
Call a flow with rate profile $f$ the Killing flow ($f = \rho$), the Hubble flow ($f = \rho_{\mathrm{H}}$, slices in $(0,1)$) or the rapidity flow ($f = 1$). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] each flow has exactly one KMS state at each $\beta \neq 0$ on each admissible finite set of slices, the product of slice Gibbs states with rapidity periods $\beta f(r_{k})$; two flows have a common KMS state (at nonzero inverse temperatures) only if their profiles are proportional on the slices, and $\rho_{\mathrm{H}}/\rho = \tau\sqrt{r/(1-r)}$ is strictly increasing on $(0,1)$, so no state on two or more slices is KMS for two of the three flows;
\item[(ii)] the Killing-flow KMS state whose horizon slice has rapidity period $2\pi$ (inverse temperature $2\pi/\kappa$) has rapidity period $2\pi/r^{2}$ on the slice $r$ and, for $r < 1$, local Hubble-normalised inverse temperature $6\pi\sqrt{1-r}/(r^{2}S(r)) = 6\pi r^{-2}(1 - \tfrac45 r + O(r^{2}))$, which diverges at the exceptional set: the horizon's state extends uniquely along the Killing flow and tends to the ground state there;
\item[(iii)] every Hubble-flow KMS state at $\beta_{\mathrm{H}} > 0$ has rapidity period $\beta_{\mathrm{H}}\rho_{\mathrm{H}}(r) \to \infty$ as $r \to 1$, so none restricts, on slices approaching the horizon, to the horizon's state with period $2\pi$; the one at $\beta_{\mathrm{H}} = 6\pi$ has rapidity period $2\pi(1 + \tfrac45 r + O(r^{2}))$, tending to $2\pi$ only at the exceptional set;
\item[(iv)] the rapidity-flow KMS state at $\beta = 2\pi$ (period $2\pi$ on every slice) restricts to the horizon's state and has local Hubble-normalised inverse temperature $2\pi/\rho_{\mathrm{H}}(r) \to 6\pi$ and local Killing inverse temperature $4\pi r^{2}$; by (i) it is KMS for neither the Killing nor the Hubble flow.
\end{enumerate}
So under (C) the KMS state of the completed pair does not extend to a KMS state of the Hubble-normalised holonomy flow, and its extension along the Killing holonomy flow reads infinite inverse temperature at the exceptional set. The value $\beta_{\mathrm{H}} = 6\pi$ is the inverse temperature at the exceptional set only of a Hubble-KMS state with rapidity period $2\pi$ imposed there (not the horizon's state), or as the local value of the slice-wise state of (iv), which is KMS for neither geometric flow. In particular, under (C) the conditional sentence of Proposition \claimref{singularity.modular-flow-boundary-modes-holonomy-universal}(ii) fails when the $t$-line holonomy flow is parametrised by Killing time (by (ii)) and is vacuous when it is parametrised by the Hubble-normalised parameter (by (iii)); its value $6\pi$ belongs to the state of (iv). The failure is located in the profile $\rho_{\mathrm{H}}/\rho$, whose running $\tfrac45 r$ separates (iii) from (iv) at first order and whose divergence at the horizon excludes the Hubble flow there.

synthesis.de-sitter-temperature-mirror-a.formal:
Let $\Lambda > 0$ and let $T_{\Lambda} = \sqrt{Q(I_{\Lambda})}/2\pi$ be the temperature of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.de-sitter-temperature-frozen-point-norm}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(b)] The mirror's pair has the same rate and the same occupation boost, its ladder reversed (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.cosmological-horizon-completed-pair}(v)). So if (P) (Premise \claimref{singularity.p-univalence}) and (R$_{\Lambda}$) (Premise \claimref{synthesis.r-lambda-regularity-cosmological-horizon}) hold for the mirror's modes, which see the same Euclidean section because both towers are soldered to one manifold (Theorem \claimref{odd-extensions.two-towers-one-curved-universe}), the mirror's horizon temperature is the same $T_{\Lambda}$. This is a temperature of the horizon and says nothing about the ratio of the two sectors' plasma temperatures.
\item[(c)] The one conversion factor that the algebra does not supply (Theorem \claimref{singularity.rate-rigidity-temperature-not-algebra-intrinsic}) is here $\kappa_{\Lambda} = \sqrt{Q(I_{\Lambda})}$, the frozen point's norm, which under the action as written is a parameter of the laws (Proposition \claimref{dark-energy.under-action-written-norm-parameter-laws}), so $T_{\Lambda}$ is undetermined within the series exactly as $\Lambda$ is.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.imported-haag-local-normality.formal:
Let $\mathcal{O} \mapsto \mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})$ be the net of local algebras of a quantum field theory over the bounded (relatively compact, open) regions $\mathcal{O}$ of spacetime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Local normality; Haag.) The physical states are required to be locally normal with respect to the vacuum: for every bounded region $\mathcal{O}$ the restriction of a physical state to $\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})$ is a normal state of the vacuum representation restricted to $\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})$, equivalently its GNS representation restricted to $\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})$ is quasi-equivalent to the vacuum representation restricted there. This is a principle of the theory, selecting the physical states, and not a theorem.
\item[(b)] (Local quasi-equivalence in a gravitational background; Haag, Narnhofer and Stein.) For a quantum field in a gravitational background, the principle of local definiteness includes local quasi-equivalence: for any two physical states $\omega_{1}$, $\omega_{2}$ and every relatively compact open region $\mathcal{O}$, the representations $\pi_{\omega_{1}}$ and $\pi_{\omega_{2}}$ restricted to $\mathcal{A}(\mathcal{O})$ are quasi-equivalent. With a condition on the short-distance scaling limit of the state at the horizon, the Hawking temperature of a Killing horizon follows from it.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Haag1996, HaagNarnhoferStein1984}.

synthesis.imported-local-quasi-equivalen.formal:
Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) whose derived metric is globally hyperbolic, read on several charts where its global structure needs them, and let a quasi-free state be Hadamard as in Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-microlocal-hadamard-condition}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Verch; the Klein-Gordon field.) The GNS representations of any two quasi-free Hadamard states of the Weyl algebra of the Klein-Gordon field are locally quasi-equivalent: their restrictions to the algebra of any open relatively compact region are quasi-equivalent. On ultrastatic spacetimes they are moreover locally primary and locally definite, and the local von Neumann algebras of sufficiently small regions are factors of type III$_{1}$.
\item[(b)] (D'Antoni and Hollands; the Dirac field.) The GNS representations of any two quasi-free Hadamard states of the free Dirac field are quasi-equivalent on the field algebra $\mathfrak{F}(\mathcal{O})$ of any open relatively compact region $\mathcal{O}$ (Theorem III.1); the local quasi-equivalence is a local unitary equivalence, and the local von Neumann algebras are factors of type III$_{1}$ (Theorem III.2). The split property holds in the representation of any quasi-free Hadamard state.
\end{enumerate}
So for these free fields the local quasi-equivalence of physical states, which Fact \claimref{synthesis.imported-haag-local-normality}(b) states as a principle, is a theorem once the physical states are taken to be the quasi-free Hadamard states. \emph{Provenance:} \cite{Verch1994}; \cite[Thms.~III.1, III.2]{DAntoniHollands2006}.

synthesis.imported-loewner-theorem-and-y.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Loewner.) Let $I$ be an open interval of $\R$ and $f : I \to \R$. The following are equivalent: $f$ is operator monotone on $I$ ($A \le B$ implies $f(A) \le f(B)$ for self-adjoint matrices of every size with spectra in $I$); every Loewner matrix $\big[(f(x_{k}) - f(x_{l}))/(x_{k} - x_{l})\big]_{k,l}$, with $f'(x_{k})$ on the diagonal, for finitely many distinct points $x_{k} \in I$, is positive semidefinite (for $f$ continuously differentiable); $f$ is the restriction to $I$ of a Pick function, analytic on the upper half-plane with nonnegative imaginary part there, which continues analytically across $I$ into the lower half-plane by reflection.
\item[(b)] (Yafaev.) Let $h$ be a distribution on $(0, \infty)$ in the class of the source, defining the Hankel form $\int_{0}^{\infty}\int_{0}^{\infty}h(t + s)f(s)\overline{f(t)}\,ds\,dt$ on test functions $f$ on $(0, \infty)$. If the form is nonnegative, then $h(t) = \int_{0}^{\infty}e^{-t\lambda}\,dm(\lambda)$ with a positive measure $m$ of at most polynomial growth. More generally the numbers of positive and negative eigenvalues of the Hankel operator equal those of multiplication by its sign-function $s$, so the operator is nonnegative exactly when $s \ge 0$.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Loewner1934}; \cite[Theorem 4.7 (arXiv), Theorem 4.9 (journal)]{Yafaev2015}.

synthesis.imported-microlocal-hadamard-c.formal:
Let $(\hb, \Omega)$ be a gravitational field on flat spacetime (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) whose derived metric $g$ is globally hyperbolic, read on several charts where its global structure needs them. For nonzero covectors write $(x_{1}, \xi_{1}) \sim (x_{2}, \xi_{2})$ if $x_{1} \neq x_{2}$ lie on a null geodesic $\gamma$ of $g$, $\xi_{1}$ is cotangent to $\gamma$ at $x_{1}$ and $\xi_{2}$ is its parallel transport along $\gamma$, or if $x_{1} = x_{2}$ and $\xi_{1} = \xi_{2}$ is null. For a distribution $D$ on $M \times M$ write $\mathrm{WF}'(D) = \{(x_{1}, \xi_{1}; x_{2}, \xi_{2}) : (x_{1}, \xi_{1}; x_{2}, -\xi_{2}) \in \mathrm{WF}(D)\}$, and let $C^{\pm} = \{(x_{1}, \xi_{1}; x_{2}, \xi_{2}) : (x_{1}, \xi_{1}) \sim (x_{2}, \xi_{2}),\ \xi_{1}\ \text{future-directed (}+\text{), past-directed (}-\text{)}\}$, in the Fourier convention of the sources.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Radzikowski.) For the two-point distribution $\Lambda$ of a quasi-free state of the neutral Klein-Gordon field, the global Hadamard condition of Kay and Wald holds if and only if $\Lambda$ satisfies the wave front set spectral condition, $\mathrm{WF}'(\Lambda) = C^{+}$ (equivalently, the associated Feynman propagator is a distinguished parametrix). The Hadamard form is preserved by Cauchy evolution, by propagation of singularities. Any two such Hadamard two-point distributions differ by a smooth function on $M \times M$ (added in version 2).
\item[(b)] (Hollands; the Dirac field.) For a quasi-free state $\omega$ of the Dirac field with the two two-point functions $\mathcal{G}^{(+)}(h, f) = \omega(\Psi(f)\bar\Psi(h))$ and $\mathcal{G}^{(-)}(h, f) = \omega(\bar\Psi(h)\Psi(f))$, $\omega$ is Hadamard if $\mathrm{WF}'(\mathcal{G}^{(\pm)}) = C^{(\pm)}$. The conditions on $\mathcal{G}^{(+)}$ and $\mathcal{G}^{(-)}$ are in general independent and are imposed separately. Any two Hadamard states differ by a smooth piece: the differences of their two-point functions are smooth kernels.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{Radzikowski1996}; \cite[Def.~IV.1, Thm.~IV.2]{Hollands2001}; for the scalar smooth-difference statement \cite[Secs.~2.3, 3.2]{Fewster2012}. Not part of this Fact: a restriction of a Hadamard state of a four-dimensional field to the algebra of one null generator; the store's generator condition is an analogue posed directly on that algebra.

synthesis.imported-modular-conjugation-o.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Bisognano-Wichmann for conformal nets; Brunetti, Guido and Longo.) For a local net of von Neumann algebras on the circle, covariant under the M\"obius group with positive energy and with a unique invariant vacuum $\Omega$, the modular group of the algebra of an interval $I$ with respect to $\Omega$ is the one-parameter M\"obius subgroup fixing the endpoints of $I$, with the modular parameter rescaled by $2\pi$, and the modular conjugation implements the M\"obius reflection fixing the endpoints of $I$, mapping the algebra of $I$ onto that of its complement (Haag duality). On the line, for the half-line $(a, \infty)$, the modular group is the dilation about $a$ and the conjugation implements $u \mapsto 2a - u$.
\item[(b)] (Twisted duality; Baumgaertel, Jurke and Lledo.) For the self-dual CAR algebra over a Hilbert space with antiunitary involution, in any Fock representation, and a closed subspace $q$ invariant under the involution, the von Neumann algebras satisfy $\mathcal{M}(q)' = Z\mathcal{M}(q^{\perp})Z^{*}$, with $Z = (1 + i\Theta)/(1 + i)$ the Klein twist built from the grading $\Theta$; the graph of the modular operator has an explicit form, which applies to free fermionic nets.
\item[(c)] (The field-level form for the chiral Fermi field.) For the free chiral Fermi field $\psi$ on the line, $\CAR(L^{2}(\R)\otimes K)$, in its vacuum, the gauge-invariant quasi-free state with symbol $\chi_{(-\infty, 0)}(P)\otimes\id_{K}$, $P = i\,d/du$, the modular conjugation $J_{a}$ of the algebra of the half-line $(a, \infty)$ acts on the fields as the Klein-twisted charge-conjugating reflection about $a$: $J_{a}\psi(f)J_{a} = c'\,Z\psi^{*}(f\circ r_{a})Z^{*}$ for $f$ supported in $(a, \infty)$, $r_{a}(u) = 2a - u$, with a constant phase $c'$ independent of $f$ and $a$. It is the second quantisation, twisted by $Z$, of the one-particle modular conjugation, as in (a) for local nets; the value of $c'$ depends on the conventions and is not part of this Fact.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{BGL1993}; \cite{BJL2002}; clause (c) and the circle form of (a) cited from memory.

synthesis.imported-null-plane-restrictio.formal:
On the flat gravitational field ($\hb = 1$, $\Omega = 0$; Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}) let $N$ be a null plane.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] (Leutwyler, Klauder and Streit.) The algebra generated by a free field on $N$ (or on a lightlike slab) is irreducible, and the free fields of different masses $m \ge 0$ restricted to $N$ are unitarily equivalent; on spacelike planes the free fields of different masses are inequivalent.
\item[(b)] (Wall.) For a free scalar field of mass $m \ge 0$ the restriction to a null surface, with its null stress $T_{kk} = \tfrac12(\nabla_{k}\Phi)^{2}$ and the commutator of the restricted field, involves no mass; the mass enters only the zero modes and finite-energy constraints. For the Dirac field one half of the spinor is free data on the null surface and the other half is fixed by a constraint in which the mass appears. The vacuum is the ground state of the null energy on the surface.
\end{enumerate}
\emph{Provenance:} \cite{LeutwylerKlauderStreit1970}; \cite[Secs.~3.2, 4.1]{Wall2012}.

synthesis.incidence-vacua-entanglement-l.formal:
In the setting of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.incidence-vacua-thermofield-real-capacity}: $\Cl(2,4)$ on its Fock module with every generator unitary, a point $x = te_{1} + re_{2}$ ($r \geq 0$ after a spatial rotation), $u = t - r$, $v = t + r$, and the distinguished vacua $\Omega_{x} = (1 - u\,a^{*}b^{*})\ket{0}_{+}$, $\Psi_{x} = (b^{*} - v\,a^{*})\ket{0}_{-}$ of the incidence line (Proposition \claimref{one-relation.spacetime-manifold-vacuum-lines}).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Profile.)} The entanglement entropy of the ascent mode with the boost qubit $b = b(x)$ is $S_{\Omega}(x) = s(u)$ in $\Omega_{x}$ and $S_{\Psi}(x) = s(v)$ in $\Psi_{x}$, where $s(w) = \ln(1 + w^{2}) - n\ln w^{2}$ and $n = w^{2}/(1 + w^{2})$ is the ascent occupation. The partner $b(x)$ is the boost-qubit mode of the $(t, r)$ plane through $x$, so it depends on the spatial direction of $x$: the profile compares entanglements with different partners at different points. Since each marginal is the Gibbs state of the fixed number operator $N$ at $e^{-\beta} = w^{2}$, the Gibbs identity for a one-parameter family of Gibbs states of one Hamiltonian gives $ds/dw = \beta\,dn/dw$, $\beta = -\ln w^{2}$; this is a property of such families, not of the incidence chart. So $s$ is stationary exactly at $w = 0$, where it vanishes, and at $w^{2} = 1$, where it is maximal, $s = \ln 2$; it increases with $|w|$ on $[0, 1]$ and decreases beyond.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(The light cones of the unit diamond.)} $\Omega_{x}$ is maximally entangled exactly on $\{|u| = 1\}$, the future light cones of $\pm e_{1}$, and $\Psi_{x}$ exactly on $\{|v| = 1\}$, their past light cones; only the union $\{|u| = 1\}\cup\{|v| = 1\}$ is the union of the light cones of the two events $\pm e_{1}$. These are the tips of the causal diamond of the unit ball $\{t = 0,\ r \leq 1\}$; on its null boundary $\Omega_{x}$ is maximally entangled on the past part ($u = -1$) and $\Psi_{x}$ on the future part ($v = 1$). On the slice $t = 0$ both entropies equal $s(r)$, zero at the centre, increasing to $\ln 2$ on the unit sphere $r = 1$, decreasing outside.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Fixed by the Hermitian structure.)} Boosts of the $(t, r)$ plane rescale $u$ and $v$ oppositely and dilations rescale both, so neither preserves the maximal set; their generators $e_{1}e_{2}$ and $e_{+}e_{-}$ are self-adjoint for the Hermitian structure, so the groups they generate are not unitary. The diamond, its radius and its rest frame $e_{1}$ are therefore determined by the Hermitian structure, and the profile depends on $x$ only through $u$ and $v$.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.point-spectrum-boost-admits-no.formal:
Let $\mathfrak{A}$ be the boundary-mode algebra of Definition \claimref{obj.hawking.boundary-mode-algebra-windows-boost-dynamics} over a countable mode set $J$ (Fact \claimref{hawking.imported-algebra-boundary-modes-car-algebra}), with window algebras $\mathfrak{A}_{F}$ and a quasi-free boost dynamics $\tau_{t}(a_{j}) = e^{-i\omega_{j}t}a_{j}$ with arbitrary real weights $\omega_{j}$ (positive, as in the Definition, or the bilateral weights $\omega_{k} = \kappa k$ of Premise \claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave}). The operative hypothesis is that the boost multiplies each generating mode by a phase, so that it has pure point spectrum on the windows; countability of the mode set alone would not suffice, since a one-particle space on which the boost has continuous spectrum also has countable bases, in which the boost is not diagonal. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] every window is $\tau$-invariant and every element of $\bigcup_{F}\mathfrak{A}_{F}$ has a relatively compact $\tau$-orbit; on a window no nonzero $P$ satisfies $[K, P] = icP$ with $c$ real and nonzero, $K = \sum_{j \in F}\omega_{j}N_{j}$;
\item[(ii)] if $(\gamma_{a})_{a \in \R}$ is a one-parameter group of $*$-automorphisms of $\mathfrak{A}$, strongly continuous ($a \mapsto \gamma_{a}(x)$ norm continuous for each $x$), with $\tau_{t}\circ\gamma_{a}\circ\tau_{-t} = \gamma_{e^{ct}a}$ for all $t, a$ and some real $c \neq 0$, then $\gamma_{a} = \id$ for every $a$;
\item[(iii)] let $\omega$ be a $\tau$-invariant state with GNS triple $(\mathcal{H}, \pi, \Omega)$ and $V_{t}$ the unitary group with $V_{t}\pi(x)\Omega = \pi(\tau_{t}(x))\Omega$. If $(U(a))_{a \in \R}$ is a strongly continuous unitary group on $\mathcal{H}$ with $V_{t}U(a)V_{-t} = U(e^{ct}a)$ for all $t, a$ and some real $c \neq 0$, then $U(a) = 1$ for every $a$. In particular, if the modular unitaries of $(\pi(\mathfrak{A})'', \Omega)$ act through the boost, $\Delta^{is} = V_{-\beta s}$ for some $\beta \neq 0$, as they do when $\omega$ is a faithful $(\tau, \beta)$-KMS state, then every unitary group satisfying the hypotheses of the abstract theorem of Fact \claimref{angle.imported-theorems-deliver-their-hypotheses} (positive generator, $U(a)\Omega = \Omega$, half-sided inclusion) is trivial, since by that theorem it satisfies $\Delta^{is}U(a)\Delta^{-is} = U(e^{-2\pi s}a)$, which is the hypothesis of (iii) with $c = 2\pi/\beta$;
\item[(iv)] for the weights $\kappa k$ the flow is periodic, $\tau_{2\pi/\kappa} = \id$, so (i) to (iii) hold with every orbit periodic; for weights in $\kappa(\Z + \tfrac12)$, $\tau_{2\pi/\kappa}$ is the parity automorphism.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently the mechanism by which the Bisognano-Wichmann and Borchers theorems produce the factor $2\pi$ cannot be realised on the windows, on the countable mode space, or on the array of (Q): a principle deriving (R) along that route must be posed on a one-particle space on which the boost has no eigenvectors spanning the relevant vectors, such as the continuum mode space of Definition \claimref{obj.hawking.continuum-mode-space} with a Hamiltonian of continuous spectrum. This result does not show that the route succeeds there.

synthesis.q-splits-maximum-entropy-affin.formal:
Let $\{\psi_{k}\}_{k \in \Z}$ be the fermion array of Theorem \claimref{singularity.bosonisation-bridge-s-wave-sector}, with currents $a_{m} := \sum_{k}\psi_{k}^{\dagger}\psi_{k+m}$, mode weights $\omega_{k}$, number operators $N_{k} = \psi_{k}^{\dagger}\psi_{k}$, $K_{k} = N_{k} - \tfrac12$ and $K_{\mathrm{geo}} = \sum_{k}\omega_{k}K_{k}$, and let $\mathcal{C}$ be the class of Corollary \claimref{singularity.chains-state-class-stability}: the even states every single-mode marginal of which is the Gibbs state of $K_{k}$ at $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa$, with occupation $n_{k} = (e^{\beta\omega_{k}} + 1)^{-1}$. Premise \claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave} has two clauses: (Q-qf) the joint state is the gauge-invariant quasi-free state with $\langle\psi_{k}^{\dagger}\psi_{l}\rangle = \delta_{kl}n_{k}$; (Q-w) $\omega_{k} = \kappa k$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (the quasi-free clause is a maximum-entropy selection) on every finite window, for $\varrho \in \mathcal{C}$ with product of marginals $\sigma$, one has $S(\sigma) - S(\varrho) = C(\varrho) = \rel{\varrho}{\sigma}$, with $C$ the total correlation of Corollary \claimref{singularity.deficit-carried-quanta-correlations}, and the following are equivalent: (a) $\varrho$ satisfies (Q-qf); (b) $\varrho = \sigma$; (c) $\varrho$ satisfies the geometric modular condition (G); (d) $C(\varrho) = 0$; (e) $\varrho$ has the largest von Neumann entropy in $\mathcal{C}$. Since $\mathcal{C}$ contains correlated states, (Q-qf) does not follow from (P) and (R); given them it is the maximum-entropy rule at the marginals (R) fixes, equivalently (G) in the stationary idealisation;
\item[(ii)] (the affine law) every current $a_{m}$, $m \neq 0$, is a normal mode of the boost flow, $e^{itK_{\mathrm{geo}}}a_{m}e^{-itK_{\mathrm{geo}}} = e^{-i\lambda_{m}t}a_{m}$ for all $t$, if and only if the weights are affine, $\omega_{k} = ck + d$; then $\lambda_{m} = cm$, and the linear span of the currents is invariant under the flow exactly in this case;
\item[(iii)] (the Planck clause holds for every affine law) for affine weights with $c > 0$ and the symbol $n_{k} = (e^{\beta(ck + d)} + 1)^{-1}$, $\sum_{k \in \Z}n_{k+m}(1 - n_{k}) = m/(e^{\beta cm} - 1)$ for every $m > 0$ and every offset $d$: the Bose-Einstein occupancy at temperature $1/\beta$ and frequency $cm$ holds for every affine law, so clause (3) of Theorem \claimref{singularity.bosonisation-bridge-s-wave-sector} fixes neither $d$ nor $c$; that it requires the affine form rests on reading each current as a normal mode of the boost flow, which is (ii);
\item[(iv)] (the two free data) for affine weights and an integer $k_{0}$, the particle-hole map $\psi_{k} \mapsto \psi_{k_{0} - k}^{\dagger}$ commutes with the boost flow, and carries the quasi-free Gibbs state at each $\beta$ to itself, if and only if $ck_{0} + 2d = 0$. Up to relabelling the array there are exactly two such classes: the sea at a site ($d = 0$: one mode of zero weight per array, with $n_{0} = \tfrac12$, the flow at time $2\pi/c$ the identity), which is (Q)'s choice, and the sea between sites ($d = c/2$: no zero-weight mode, the flow at time $2\pi/c$ the parity automorphism $\psi \mapsto -\psi$). In either class the one remaining datum is the dimensionless slope $y := \beta c$, the ratio of the KMS period to the recurrence time $2\pi/c$ of the flow, multiplied by $2\pi$; (Q-w) sets $y = 2\pi$, the value at which the recurrence time of the array's flow equals the KMS period;
\item[(v)] (rotations) let the counted modes be $\psi_{k,a}$ on $\ell^{2}(W) \otimes \C^{n}$, with the principal $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ of Theorem \claimref{scrambling.principality-indifference-sphere} on the second factor and the array index on the first. A quasi-free number-preserving generator with weights $\omega_{k,a}$ commutes with the second-quantised rotations if and only if $\omega_{k,a} = \omega_{k}$ does not depend on $a$; with (ii) in each channel, rotation equivariance and normal-mode currents together give $\omega_{k,a} = ck + d$, and the quasi-free state with symbol $\operatorname{diag}(n_{k}) \otimes \id$ is rotation invariant. At each array level the $n$ modes are a principal mode space of common weight $ck + d$, as Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-common-weight-particle-hole}(i) requires; the conflict of its clause (iv) arises exactly when a window of the array index is placed on the magnetic index. In the class with the sea at a site, the level $k = 0$ is a principal mode space at zero weight, maximally mixed and invariant under the particle-hole map of that theorem, the equality case of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}(ii).
\end{enumerate}
So (Q) is the conjunction of a maximum-entropy rule, an affine weight law (forced if each current is a normal mode of the boost flow, as the Planck clause reads it), and two choices the framework does not make: the offset class and the value $y = 2\pi$. At $y = 2\pi$ the entropy of one array is the value $\ln 2 + \varepsilon$ of Theorem \claimref{synthesis.residue-from-mode-entropy}(vi) in the class with the sea at a site; its value in the class with the sea between sites is the subject of a result filed alongside, synthesis.half-integer-weight-array-carries-half-ln2.

synthesis.r-le-i-independent-spectrum-co.formal:
Model a window by $\nu$ fermionic modes $a_{k}$ with their Killing flows $\alpha^{k}_{t} = \operatorname{Ad}(e^{it\omega_{k}K_{k}})$, $K_{k} = N_{k} - \tfrac12$, $\omega_{k} > 0$, a state $\omega$, a dynamics generator $H = H^{*}$ in the window algebra, and an exterior channel map $a^{\mathrm{out}}_{k} = \sum_{j}T_{kj}a_{j} + (\text{vacuum-fed terms})$, with $T$ a contraction on the channel space. Read the premises as: (R) each single-mode marginal of $\omega$ is $(\alpha^{k}, p)$-KMS at $p = 2\pi/\kappa$, as in Premise \claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus}, equivalently its Boltzmann factor is $q_{k} = e^{-p\omega_{k}}$; (L$_E$) $H$ commutes with every $N_{k}$, as in Premise \claimref{singularity.l-boundary-mode-locality}; (I) $T$ is diagonal, so there is no cross-channel mixing: each channel feeds its own asymptotic channel, possibly attenuated by a vacuum-fed (greybody) loss, as in Premise \claimref{hawking.premise-i-interface-identification}; and the unique-ground-state condition (S) $\omega$ is the unique ground state of $H$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Independence.)} On two modes: (M1) $H = \omega_{0}N_{0} + \omega_{1}N_{1}$, the product Gibbs state and $T = 1$ satisfy (R), (L$_E$), (I), with $\omega$ stationary for $H$; (M2) the same $H$ with the vacuum $\ket{00}$ and $T = 1$ satisfies (L$_E$), (I) and (S) but not (R), at any Boltzmann factor; (M3) the pairing generator below with its ground state and $T = 1$ satisfies (R), (I) and (S) but not (L$_E$); (M4) the state and generator of (M1) with a beam splitter $T$ of nonzero mixing angle and $q_{0} \ne q_{1}$ satisfy (R) and (L$_E$) but not (I). So none of (R), (L$_E$), (I) follows from the other two.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Pairing realises (R) with (S) at every Boltzmann factor.)} For $0 < t < 1$ and $g > 0$ let $H_{t} = g\bigl[\tfrac{1 - t^{2}}{2t}(N_{0} + N_{1}) + a_{0}^{*}a_{1}^{*} + a_{1}a_{0}\bigr]$. Its unique ground state is $\ket{00} - t\ket{11}$ (with $\ket{11} = a_{0}^{*}a_{1}^{*}\ket{00}$), of energy $-gt$, separated from the rest of the spectrum, and both single-mode marginals are Gibbs with Boltzmann factor $t^{2}$; so for equal frequencies (R) holds with $t = e^{-p\omega/2}$, while $H_{t}$ does not commute with $N_{0}$.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(The unique-ground-state condition separates.)} Under (S), (L$_E$) excludes (R) (Proposition \claimref{angle.within-supplied-class-spectrum-condition-selects}(i): the unique ground state of a number-diagonal generator is a number basis state, with pure marginals), and by (ii) (R) is realised under (S) only outside (L$_E$). Hence no premise that selects the window state as the unique ground state of the window dynamics can yield (R) and (L$_E$) together. Uniqueness is needed: without it the exclusion fails, since the number-diagonal $H = (N_{0} - N_{1})^{2}$ has a degenerate ground space containing a state with thermal marginals (Proposition \claimref{angle.within-supplied-class-spectrum-condition-selects}(ii)).
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.state-selection-r-and-le-again.formal:
Let a window of $n$ boundary modes, completed pairs $(a_{k}, a_{k}^{*})$ obeying the canonical anticommutation relations (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.boundary-mode-space-matrix}), carry the Killing flow $\alpha_{t} = \operatorname{Ad}(e^{it\sum_{k}\omega_{k}K_{k}})$, $K_{k} = N_{k} - \tfrac12$ (Proposition \claimref{hawking.modular-structure-completed-pair}), with period $p = 2\pi/\kappa$ and $Q_{k} = e^{\omega_{k}p}$. For mode operators $a, b$ write $F_{a,b}(t) = \omega(a\,\alpha_{t}(b))$, a finite sum of exponentials in $t$. Premise (R) of the state-selection note (its Premise 5.1, now retired from the store; its statement is recorded with \claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus}) asks that the state's two-point functions, continued in the rapidity of the boost flow, be single valued and regular at its fixed locus; T05's Premise (R) (\claimref{singularity.r-regularity-fixed-locus}) asks that each single-mode correlator continue to the Euclidean section with its two orderings glued across the cut, $F_{a,b}(t + ip) = \omega(\alpha_{t}(b)\,a)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] (one mode, three readings) on a mode of nonzero weight: regularity alone is vacuous (every $F_{a,b}$ is entire); single-valuedness read as strict periodicity, $F_{a,b}(t + ip) = F_{a,b}(t)$ for both orderings of the ladder, holds for no state (it forces the occupancy to be both $1$ and $0$); single-valuedness read with the orderings glued, as T05 reads it, holds exactly when the occupancy is $n = 1/(Q + 1)$, which is T05's (R) on that mode; the ladder correlators do not see $\omega(a)$, and with $\omega(a) = 0$, as (P) (\claimref{singularity.p-univalence}) gives, the marginal is the Gibbs state at $\beta = p$ (Theorem \claimref{singularity.kms-uniqueness-factor}). At zero weight strict periodicity is vacuous and the glued reading forces $n = \tfrac12$ at every period;
\item[(ii)] (the glued reading on the whole window) write (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) for the glued reading imposed on every two-point function of mode operators. On mixed correlators, $i \ne j$, it reads $Q^{\mp 1}\omega(a_{i}b_{j}) = -\omega(a_{i}b_{j})$ for $b_{j} \in \{a_{j}^{*}, a_{j}\}$, so (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) holds if and only if T05's (R) holds on every mode and every inter-mode two-point function vanishes;
\item[(iii)] (strictly between) T05's (R) with the two-point clause of (Q) (\claimref{singularity.q-joint-quasi-freeness-s-wave}) implies (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$), and (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) implies T05's (R); neither converse holds. On two modes with Gibbs marginals: adding a coherence $c \ne 0$ between $\ket{10}$ and $\ket{01}$ gives an even state satisfying T05's (R) with $\omega(a_{1}a_{2}^{*}) = -c$, so (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) fails; adding instead a density correlation $d \ne 0$ gives an even state satisfying (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) with $\omega(N_{1}N_{2}) - n_{1}n_{2} = d$, so it is not quasi-free and (Q) fails;
\item[(iv)] (the note's rate theorem) on each mode, the Gibbs form at $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa$ with occupancy in $(0,1)$, hence faithful marginals, follows from (R$_{\mathrm{reg}}$) and (P) by (i), without the geometric modular condition, as T05's Theorem \claimref{singularity.rate-discharge-hypothesis-iii} obtains it from T05's (R); the single-mode conclusions of the note's Theorem \claimref{state-selection.rate-discharge-hypothesis-iii} therefore need only T05's (R);
\item[(v)] (the two (L$_E$)) T05's Premise \claimref{singularity.l-boundary-mode-locality} and the note's Premise 7.3 (now retired from the store as a restatement of T05's) generate the dynamics by the boost bivectors $\sigma_{k}$ and their products, the abelian algebra $\mathcal{A}_{\sigma}$ of dimension $2^{n}$, which commutes with every $N_{k}$, contains the cross-point products $\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}$, and excludes the hopping $a_{j}^{*}a_{k}$ and the pairing $a_{j}^{*}a_{k}^{*}$ ($j \ne k$). The note's clause that no generator couples the ladder at one point to the ladder at another, read as excluding every generator that involves two points, would exclude $\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}$, which its own first clause admits and its Proposition 7.4, merged into \claimref{singularity.number-diagonal-dynamics-models-scrambling-derived}, uses (the controlled-$Z$); read as excluding transfer of occupation, it is T05's clause. The two premises name one generator class.
\end{enumerate}
So the note's (L$_E$) restates T05's, and the note's (R), in its one satisfiable contentful reading on the boundary-mode algebra, is T05's (R) plus vanishing inter-mode two-point functions.
